WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.29
This is journey one of Hewn, the software factory: build a new product.

00:00:06.29 --> 00:00:10.06
An Owner types a product direction into Mission Control,

00:00:10.06 --> 00:00:14.92
and Hewn turns it into a working, tested product in a GitHub repository.

00:00:14.92 --> 00:00:19.03
It runs eleven stages and asks nobody anything along the way.

00:00:19.03 --> 00:00:22.40
This video walks through the whole implementation,

00:00:22.40 --> 00:00:25.50
from the form to the last merged pull request.

00:00:26.65 --> 00:00:28.38
Journey one is for Owners.

00:00:28.38 --> 00:00:31.37
They want a product, not a project to manage.

00:00:31.37 --> 00:00:35.28
Hewn takes a name, an idea, an optional stack and a budget,

00:00:35.28 --> 00:00:40.33
and from there it works alone: it writes the documents, designs the screens,

00:00:40.33 --> 00:00:44.85
scaffolds the code, plans the work and ships it in small increments.

00:00:45.20 --> 00:00:47.51
The pipeline has eleven stages.

00:00:47.51 --> 00:00:52.80
Bootstrap, idea, designs, design system, stack, requirements, scaffold,

00:00:52.80 --> 00:00:57.13
detailed designs, user journeys, estimates and increments.

00:00:57.13 --> 00:01:02.72
Each stage runs on a worker, and each one ends in a commit to the product's

00:01:02.72 --> 00:01:03.54
repository.

00:01:04.69 --> 00:01:06.56
Three parts work together.

00:01:06.56 --> 00:01:10.37
Mission Control is the Angular app the Owner watches.

00:01:10.37 --> 00:01:14.47
The Hewn API is a dot net service that owns the pipeline,

00:01:14.47 --> 00:01:17.42
the database and every GitHub credential.

00:01:17.42 --> 00:01:22.74
Workers are Node processes that lease one stage at a time and do the work.

00:01:23.09 --> 00:01:27.79
A worker leases a run, sends heartbeats, and checkpoints after each step.

00:01:27.79 --> 00:01:33.06
Every request carries the lease's epoch, so a worker that lost its lease is fenced

00:01:33.06 --> 00:01:36.60
out and can never write after another worker took over.

00:01:36.95 --> 00:01:39.64
Workers never hold a GitHub credential.

00:01:39.64 --> 00:01:44.20
Every write goes through the API's broker: commits, pull requests,

00:01:44.20 --> 00:01:46.21
merges and branch protection.

00:01:46.21 --> 00:01:51.53
Each is keyed by run and step, so a resumed worker gets the first result back

00:01:51.53 --> 00:01:53.26
instead of writing twice.

00:01:53.26 --> 00:01:58.72
And a commit policy decides, per stage, which branches and paths that stage may

00:01:58.72 --> 00:01:59.13
write.

00:02:00.28 --> 00:02:03.42
Stage zero creates or connects the repository,

00:02:03.42 --> 00:02:08.54
and copies in the Greenlit skills the agents follow, pinned in a lock file.

00:02:08.89 --> 00:02:12.74
Stages one, four, five, seven and eight write documents.

00:02:12.74 --> 00:02:17.20
The agent writes the brief, the stack decision, the requirements,

00:02:17.20 --> 00:02:20.16
the detailed designs and the user journeys.

00:02:20.16 --> 00:02:24.76
Then a validator checks each document before anything is committed.

00:02:24.76 --> 00:02:29.09
If it fails, the run fails, and nothing reaches the repository.

00:02:29.44 --> 00:02:33.84
Stages two and three design the product through Magic Patterns.

00:02:33.84 --> 00:02:39.29
Stage two creates one design per screen in four states, and exports the mocks.

00:02:39.29 --> 00:02:44.19
Stage three extracts a design system, with tokens and component pages,

00:02:44.19 --> 00:02:47.96
and checks that every colour the mocks use is a token.

00:02:48.31 --> 00:02:51.83
Stage six generates the scaffold for the chosen stack.

00:02:51.83 --> 00:02:56.47
It merges it through a pull request once continuous integration passes,

00:02:56.47 --> 00:03:00.32
and then makes those checks required on the default branch.

00:03:00.67 --> 00:03:04.19
Stage nine estimates every design in ideal days.

00:03:04.19 --> 00:03:10.06
It defers what is oversized, out of scope, or on no journey, each with a reason,

00:03:10.06 --> 00:03:14.90
and plans a backlog of increments in journey and dependency order.

00:03:16.05 --> 00:03:19.93
Stage ten delivers that backlog, one increment at a time.

00:03:19.93 --> 00:03:24.90
Each increment starts on a feature branch from the latest default branch.

00:03:24.90 --> 00:03:27.97
The first commit holds only acceptance tests,

00:03:27.97 --> 00:03:31.03
each tracing to the increment's requirements.

00:03:31.03 --> 00:03:33.07
A guard rejects anything else.

00:03:33.42 --> 00:03:37.68
The new tests run once, and the failing result is recorded as red.

00:03:37.68 --> 00:03:42.96
Then the agent implements in iterations, and a fixed gate decides when it is done:

00:03:42.96 --> 00:03:47.47
backend tests, frontend lint and unit tests, and the end to end suite.

00:03:47.47 --> 00:03:51.01
After ten failing iterations, the increment is blocked,

00:03:51.01 --> 00:03:55.33
and work moves on to the next increment that does not depend on it.

00:03:55.68 --> 00:03:59.24
When the gate is green, the agent records a short video,

00:03:59.24 --> 00:04:01.14
and Hewn opens a pull request.

00:04:01.14 --> 00:04:04.25
Its title names the journey and the requirements.

00:04:04.25 --> 00:04:08.44
Its body has six sections, with every test command and its counts.

00:04:08.44 --> 00:04:12.69
When the checks pass, Hewn squash merges it and deletes the branch.

00:04:12.69 --> 00:04:15.61
If the default branch moved, it rebases first.

00:04:15.61 --> 00:04:20.44
Then it marks the designs complete and updates the estimates before the next

00:04:20.44 --> 00:04:21.52
increment starts.

00:04:22.67 --> 00:04:26.20
Skills are written for people, and they ask questions.

00:04:26.20 --> 00:04:28.22
Hewn never waits for an answer.

00:04:28.22 --> 00:04:33.32
Every agent session is told that nobody will reply, and it has no tool to ask.

00:04:33.32 --> 00:04:37.11
So the agent decides from the direction and the documents,

00:04:37.11 --> 00:04:42.21
and appends each decision to the decision log, with the date, stage, question,

00:04:42.21 --> 00:04:43.59
answer and rationale.

00:04:43.59 --> 00:04:48.03
A decision that changes the architecture also gets a numbered A D R.

00:04:49.18 --> 00:04:52.87
Everything was built test first, in nine pull requests.

00:04:52.87 --> 00:04:58.04
The backend has one hundred and twenty acceptance tests against the real API,

00:04:58.04 --> 00:04:59.72
with an in memory GitHub.

00:04:59.72 --> 00:05:03.75
The worker has one hundred and nine tests against fake APIs.

00:05:03.75 --> 00:05:09.45
The end to end suites drive Mission Control with real workers: eight behaviour tests,

00:05:09.45 --> 00:05:13.95
five accessibility tests, two visual tests and four pipeline tests.

00:05:14.30 --> 00:05:18.05
The last pipeline test is the one that matters most.

00:05:18.05 --> 00:05:21.73
It submits a small direction and then only watches.

00:05:21.73 --> 00:05:26.06
It passes when every stage succeeds, no dialog ever appears,

00:05:26.06 --> 00:05:29.38
and every planned increment is merged by Hewn.

00:05:30.53 --> 00:05:31.89
Here is New direction.

00:05:31.89 --> 00:05:36.40
Submit it empty, and the errors appear inline, with a summary at the top.

00:05:36.75 --> 00:05:39.31
Here is the real app with one worker.

00:05:39.31 --> 00:05:44.79
The Owner types a name and an idea that is too short, sees the error, fixes it,

00:05:44.79 --> 00:05:45.62
and submits.

00:05:45.62 --> 00:05:48.05
From there nobody touches anything.

00:05:48.05 --> 00:05:53.18
The stages advance from pending to succeeded, and the direction completes.

00:05:53.53 --> 00:05:58.82
While it runs, the project page shows each stage's status in text and icon,

00:05:58.82 --> 00:06:00.38
never by colour alone.

00:06:00.38 --> 00:06:03.28
The phone layout works in dark mode, too.

00:06:04.43 --> 00:06:08.50
This is what the stages wrote into the product's repository.

00:06:08.50 --> 00:06:12.24
These come from the scripted agent the test stack uses,

00:06:12.24 --> 00:06:14.35
so they are deliberately plain.

00:06:14.35 --> 00:06:17.61
First, the brief and a mock from Magic Patterns.

00:06:17.96 --> 00:06:22.52
Next, a design system component page and the stack decision record.

00:06:22.87 --> 00:06:25.52
Then the requirements and a detailed design.

00:06:25.87 --> 00:06:30.07
Then the user journeys and the estimates, with the roadmap of increments.

00:06:30.42 --> 00:06:33.41
Here is one increment going from red to green.

00:06:33.41 --> 00:06:36.92
The acceptance test came first, with its trace header.

00:06:36.92 --> 00:06:40.56
The red run failed it, the first iteration made it pass,

00:06:40.56 --> 00:06:44.27
and the pull request was opened and merged automatically.

00:06:44.62 --> 00:06:50.12
And this is the decision log, one entry per question an agent answered for itself.

00:06:51.27 --> 00:06:52.92
Journey one is complete.

00:06:52.92 --> 00:06:57.99
An Owner submits a direction, and Hewn runs all eleven stages to a merged,

00:06:57.99 --> 00:07:00.66
tested product, with no approval steps.

00:07:00.66 --> 00:07:05.80
Every write goes through the API's broker, every stage's output is checked,

00:07:05.80 --> 00:07:07.93
and every decision is recorded.

00:07:07.93 --> 00:07:13.55
Next come the journeys that let Owners watch and steer: budgets, pause and resume,

00:07:13.55 --> 00:07:16.02
and the backlog and increment pages.
